<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Youzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Youzhou was a historical prefecture of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> centred on the city of Ji, on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. A frontier stronghold against the steppe peoples, it was the base of the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> and later of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-rengong">Liu Rengong</a> and his son's short-lived Yan state. In 938 it passed to the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> and became their southern capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/youzhou.jpg" alt="Youzhou" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_orientaux_140.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Several ancient texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> list You among the Nine Provinces into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a> was said to have divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, though the Book of Xia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Classic of History</a> does not include it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> wrote that Shun created it from part of Yanzhou. These texts treat it as roughly the land of the State of Yan. As a real unit it was founded in 106 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> divided the Western Han into 13 province-sized prefectures under inspectors. You covered eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> with 173 counties along the northern frontier, from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> through north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the end of the Han the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a> briefly seized Ji in 184, and the prefecture passed through the hands of the warlords Liu Yu, Gongsun Zan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a>, Yuan Xi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, who defeated the Wuhuan in 207. Under the Kingdom of Wei a governor built the Lilingyan irrigation system in 250, which greatly improved farming around Ji. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period the prefecture was held in turn by Wang Jun, the Later Zhao, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, whose ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> moved his capital to Ji in 352, the Former Qin, the Later Yan and the Northern Wei.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> did not use prefectures and renamed You as Zhuo Commandery, but the Tang restored it as a smaller prefecture with its capital still at Ji. The Fanyang Jiedushi, a frontier command based at Ji, became one of the three revolting garrisons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> after the Anshi Rebellion. In the Five Dynasties period the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-rengong">Liu Rengong</a> made Youzhou his base, and his regime was overthrown by the Later Tang.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a>, founder of the Later Jin, submitted to the Khitan Liao dynasty and ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>, You among them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> made it a secondary capital named Nanjing Youdu Prefecture, renamed Xijin Prefecture in 1012 and later Yanjing, and the name Youzhou was never used again.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Youzhou (ancient China)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 873 Tuoba Sigong seized <b>Youzhou</b>; for helping drive Huang Chao from Chang'an he received in 881 the four prefectures of the Dingnan Jiedushi and in 883 the imperial surname Li and the title Duke of Xia, a frontier command his house held through the Five Dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Liu Rengong was military governor of Lulong, centred on <b>Youzhou</b>, in the late Tang.</blockquote><a href="/state/yan-five-dynasties">Yan (Five Dynasties)</a></li></ul></section>
